 "voice": {
  "text": "Liberty's arm arrived years before her. In 1876, only her arm and torch had crossed the ocean, for a world's fair in Philadelphia. For a small fee, visitors climbed a ladder up to the balcony around the flame. Then the arm stood in a New York park for years, raising money for her pedestal. In Paris, her giant head went on show at the 1878 world's fair, while workers built the rest. When the pedestal money ran out in 1884, Joseph Pulitzer promised to print every donor's name in his newspaper. Turns out, by her 1886 unveiling, over a hundred thousand people had paid for her pedestal, most giving a dollar or less.",
